At first I was not sure how this challenge could be solved because the only useful things given were n and e. I knew this was RSA but I did not know how someone could decrypt something with only these values.
So I first tried to understand how RSA works and searched how RSA can be broken when only n and e are given.From that I found that n is made by multiplying two large prime numbers. The private key depends on these prime numbers. So if we can find the two prime numbers then we can calculate the other values needed for decryption.
My first thought was to directly factor n. But n was very large so doing this normally did not look practical.
I then went back to the hints. One of them mentioned randomness and another asked to notice something about n. This made me check the value of n more carefully instead of only thinking about normal factorisation.
I also tried comparing n from multiple requests because the hint mentioned doing that. I thought maybe the same prime numbers were being reused or there was some pattern in the generated values.After checking n again I noticed something unusual.
n is a even number.
Normally n is made from two large prime numbers. Since only one prime number is even it means one of the prime numbers used to create n must be 2.
That made the factorisation much easier than I expected.

